<title>INDIA: Indian soybean trading slow as 18-day protest ends.</title>
<headline>Indian soybean trading slow as 18-day protest ends.</headline>
<dateline>INDORE, India 1997-05-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Indian soybean markets reopened on Monday as traders called off an 18-day protest shutdown, but trading was slow as many farmers and traders were unaware the strike had ended.</p>
<p>They had been protesting against an increased local taxes.</p>
<p>Prices were steady.</p>
<p>Soyoil prices rose on good demand for the marriage season, but trading activity was nominal and the undertone was steady.</p>
<p>Soymeal and rapeseed extraction prices remained steady in very thin trading. Soymeal yellow was $329-330 per tonne FAS (Free Alongside Ship) at west coast Indian ports for exports. Rapeseed extraction was $129-130 per tonne FAS in bulk for export and was 4,500-4,525 rupees per tonne in FOR (Free on Rail) condition. Business volume was dull and the undertone was steady.</p>
